  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following presents the correct order of second ionization enthalpies of $\\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{N}, \\mathrm{O}$ and $\\mathrm{F}$ ?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "dKUh7",
      "text": "\nF $>\\mathrm{O}>\\mathrm{N}>\\mathrm{C}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ioNXm",
      "text": "\nO $>$ N $>$ F $>$ C\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "q37ca",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{C}>\\mathrm{N}>\\mathrm{O}>\\mathrm{F}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "8SqDN",
      "text": "\nO $>$ F $>$ N $>$ C",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "In a period the value of ionisation potential increases from left to right with breaks where the atoms have some what stable configuration. After removing an $e^{-}$ oxygen will acquire stable configuration. Hence $\\mathrm{O}^{+}$has highest second ionisation enthalpy than $\\mathrm{F}^{+}$and $\\mathrm{N}^{+}$.\n$\\therefore$ correct order is $\\mathrm{O}>\\mathrm{F}>\\mathrm{N}>\\mathrm{C}$",
    "image": null
  },
